## Shared Rules (All Languages)

### API Endpoints Consistency
```
All endpoints follow this structure:

Success (200):
{
  "status": "success",
  "data": { /* the actual data */ },
  "message": "Human-readable message"
}

Error (4xx/5xx):
{
  "status": "error",
  "data": null,
  "message": "User-friendly error message",
  "error_code": "E123_SOMETHING"
}

Never include credentials, stack traces, or internal details in error messages.
```

### Secret Management
- Environment variables: `DATABASE_URL`, `JWT_SECRET`, `ENCRYPTION_MASTER_KEY`
- Prefix secret vars with `_` if possible: `_DATABASE_PASSWORD` (helps grep)
- Use `.env.local` for development (never commit)
- Use GitHub Secrets for CI/CD
- Rotate secrets on production access compromises
- Never hardcode secrets in code
- Use constant-time comparison for token validation: `hmac.Equal()` in Go, `secrets.compare_digest()` in Python

### Database Access
- Always parameterized queries (prevent SQL injection)
- Always indexes on frequently queried columns
- Always soft delete: `deleted_at IS NULL` in WHERE clauses
- Test row-level security with actual tokens/JWT
- No N+1 queries — eager load or batch queries

### Logging
- Use structured logging: `logger.info("event", extra={...})` in Python, `s.Logger.Info("event", zap.Field(...))` in Go
- Include: timestamp, level, service, request_id, user_id
- Never log credentials, PII, or passwords
- Use appropriate log levels:
  - ERROR: something failed (bad user input, DB error, etc)
  - WARN: degraded functionality (no PK found, partial success)
  - INFO: normal operations (pipeline started, run completed)
  - DEBUG: detailed flow (phase transitions, SQL statements)

### Performance
- Add indexes before deploying new queries
- Profile before optimizing — measure, don't guess
- Use pagination: limit 1000 rows max per query
- Set timeouts on all external API calls
- Cache: static data → memory, dynamic data → Redis (if needed)

### Testing
- Write tests for new functionality
- Mock all external services (DB, APIs, file system)
- Test error paths, not just happy paths
- Test security (RLS, credential scrubbing)
- Use fixtures for test data setup/teardown
- Aim for 80%+ code coverage on critical paths

### Security
- Validate all user input on backend (never trust frontend)
- Use Supabase RLS for row-level access control
- Sanitize error messages — no internal details to users
- Use HTTPS in production
- Regularly audit dependencies for vulnerabilities
- Never run `npm install` as root

### Code Review Checklist
Before approving any PR:
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or credentials
- [ ] Error handling for all external calls
- [ ] Tests written and passing
- [ ] No N+1 queries or obvious performance issues
- [ ] Database indexes added if needed
- [ ] RLS verified (if data access changed)
- [ ] Rate limiting considered
- [ ] Timeouts set on external calls
- [ ] Backward compatibility maintained (if API change)
- [ ] Documentation updated

